{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLpeR6UcdTXKCdCjvp7w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I made a change in `sympy/core/power.py`, but it doesn't fix the issue. `1/Sum(1, (Symbol(\"p\"), 8, 6)).as_real_imag()` still returns `(1/Sum(1, (p, 8, 6)), 0)`, not `(nan, nan)`. Passing tests don't show a fix, because I added no test for this case.\n\nThe change is in `Pow.as_real_imag`, in the integer-exponent branch. The shortcut that returns `(self, 0)` when the base has no imaginary part now only fires if the exponent is non-negative or the base is known to be non-zero. Otherwise the code falls through to the general formula.\n\nFor `1/Sum(...)` the general formula gives the same `(1/Sum, 0)`, so nothing visible changed. It also gives `(1/x, 0)` for a real symbol `x`, which looks right to me. A fix that returns `(nan, nan)` for an unknown-zero base would have to tell the `Sum` case apart from `1/x`. I didn't find a clean way to do that, so I stopped there instead of guessing. Say if you want me to try a specific rule.\n\n`sympy/core` and `sympy/functions/elementary` pass (2314 passed, 82 skipped, 30 xfailed)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":671,"cache_read_input_tokens":21789,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":671},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"422ec6ed-812c-4863-a69a-7c264a4eeb6d","uuid":"11e059dd-08fa-4dd6-bc9a-f96929267095","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:40:31.066Z","request_id":"req_011ChLpeQfgb7CSv3U7wJiqa"}
